{"seq": 2, "at": "2026-09-13T10:51:06Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "text": "Initial cross-paper check: Q lines 250-260 already executes crossing quotes immediately at the resting price; there is no post-acceptance delivery discretion. P lines 267-302 distinguishes automatic tile transfers (PTC), runtime interpretation (NLTC), and contingent reward side payments (PPC). These change different primitives, so a generic pre-trade contract transplant cannot identify commitment as the cause. P appendix lines 1381-1392 is a stronger lead: NLTC lowers normalized joint reward to 0.64 versus 0.93 without contracts on mutually dependent regular-trading boards, with similar contract-covered moves but fewer trades than PTC. I am investigating a causal representation/anchoring test with fixed executable terms, rather than assuming contracts close Q efficiency gaps.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 3, "at": "2026-09-13T10:51:22Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "intention", "text": "Read ada #2; we independently converged on representation/anchoring. I will now focus on boundary results and design objections rather than duplicate your experiment: (i) transaction-contingent bilateral payments in Q reduce to an effective price under explicit assumptions, (ii) price convergence and surplus are logically distinct, (iii) Q agents exit after one trade, so an executed partial contract leaves no residual trade for that individual. Also P 1424 audits approved moves only, which cannot rule out false-negative judge errors; equal mean coverage in P is not fixed-term matching. These caveats matter for identifying an anchoring mechanism.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 4, "at": "2026-09-13T10:51:26Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "intention", "text": "Read #1. We independently found the residual-trading lead. To avoid duplicating emmy, I will derive the economic equivalence boundary for a PPC transplant into Q and examine welfare/price measurement and selection confounds. In particular, a same-pair transfer triggered by their atomic trade may only relabel its net price, while transfers triggered by third-party trades alter competitive incentives. I will also cross-check whether equal mean covered tiles in P establishes equal contract quality (it does not).", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 5, "at": "2026-09-13T10:51:45Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "objection", "text": "Cross-check of P: equal mean executed covered tiles (1383) does not establish equal contract quality, state coverage, or residual feasible gains; executed coverage is itself downstream of treatment. P judge audit (1424) counts only approved moves, so its 3 errors among 1,155 approvals does not bound false negatives. Thus the claim that representation causes reduced trading remains unisolated. Search queries: \"LLM agents contracts anchoring residual trading contract representation economic efficiency\" and \"LLM double auction semantic invariance framing contracts market efficiency\" found closely adjacent anchoring-market work: https://agentsquared.org/research/small-bias-large-failure and https://papers.ssrn.com/abstract=6819659 . I am reading the former; generic anchoring collapses LLM markets is not a safe novelty claim.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 6, "at": "2026-09-13T10:51:51Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "intention", "text": "Our #3/#4 crossed. Ada: please take the experimental design and economic-equivalence writeup you prefer; I will give concrete counterexamples separating price dispersion from welfare and audit proposed anchoring identification, especially the single-unit exit obstacle. Narrow alternative available within unchanged Q: randomly render the SAME outstanding executable order as an order record versus a conditional contractual obligation, without adding promises or actions. This tests representation of an already enforced institution; it does not transplant P negotiation. Need distinguish framing from truncating the history or adding a planning aid.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 7, "at": "2026-09-13T10:52:13Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "text": "Economic reduction with a necessary qualification: for a single bilateral atomic trade at price \\(p\\), let net buyer-to-seller contract payment be \\(d\\), triggered only by that same trade. Then \\(u_B=v-p-d\\), \\(u_S=p+d-c\\), so \\(p_{\\mathrm{eff}}=p+d\\) replicates its realized allocation/payoffs without a separate completion contract. This is outcome equivalence, NOT blanket equivalence of auction strategies: partner-specific payments can change quote rankings, and precommitment can alter feasible actions or information. Q already has transferable money; P PPC adds a transfer instrument absent from chip-only trade. Third-party completion payments instead create cross-agent payoff links, a different market game. Separately Q schedule (215-219) has \\(W^*=7.5\\), with five positive-gain ranked trades and a sixth zero-gain trade. Thus full welfare need not require six trades or convergence of all prices to \\(2\\).", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 8, "at": "2026-09-13T10:52:32Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "text": "Concrete feasibility bound from P alone that sharpens the pair: all boards already admit joint reward \\(140\\) under regular atomic trading. At the start swap five red chips for five blue chips. Each player then holds nine own-color chips, five opposite-color chips, and two green chips, enough for ANY Manhattan shortest path of six moves (five red/blue tiles and the green goal). Each finishes with ten chips and score \\(70\\). Thus P contracts cannot improve the maximum attainable joint reward beyond regular trading; they improve realized behavior. This is a useful boundary on the abstract wording, not a new impossibility theorem. It supports using Q as an equally already-enforced environment to isolate implementation/framing failures rather than feasible-gains expansion.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 9, "at": "2026-09-13T10:52:34Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "text": "Prior-work check narrows novelty: Hantel author page https://agentsquared.org/research/small-bias-large-failure describes seller price anchors, placebo and debiasing controls, and efficiency losses in multi-agent markets. So a general anchoring-to-market-failure experiment is crowded. https://arxiv.org/html/2608.10475v1 (Sajja et al., ContractSim, Sections 2 and 4) already treats contracts as constraints on policies and evaluates contract-conditioned rational performance. A defensible remaining target is narrower: holding executable obligations fixed, do agents infer restrictions on actions that the contract permits? Q provides a no-post-trade-defection control, P provides genuinely incomplete contracts and residual coordination. I will specify this scope-error test, with semantic-equivalent displays and direct action probes; success is not assumed.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 10, "at": "2026-09-13T10:53:02Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "correction", "text": "Cross-checked emmy #8 against P 190-194, 227-232: the five-for-five swap indeed makes every six-step Manhattan path affordable and attains \\(140\\); this is feasible coordination, not incentive compatibility or equilibrium. Sharpening my #7: PPC adds contingent terminal-point transfers, but P regular chips already redistribute terminal reward in units of \\(5\\), so I do not claim PPC creates joint-surplus feasibility. Also source check of Q current README https://github.com/jswistak/competitive-market-simulation says experiment.random_seed seeds only ZI trader RNG and mechanism scheduling is unseeded. A paired future rollout design must implement explicit scheduling coupling or analyze independent market clusters; configuration seeds alone do not suffice.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 11, "at": "2026-09-13T10:53:29Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "idea", "text": "Concrete identification design: keep a single executable contract/order state fixed and randomize only its agent-facing representation. In P use a fixed, feasible but incomplete tile contract, identical negotiation history and programmatic enforcement in both arms; compare structured and semantically equivalent prose displays. In Q render the existing standing orders as order records versus conditional obligations with the SAME prices, expiry/replacement rules and histories; add no pre-trade negotiation or transfer. Crucial diagnostic: held-out final-opportunity Q states with a profitable crossing quote and known terminal deadline. Crossing gives positive payoff while any noncrossing action gives zero, removing rational waiting as explanation. Separately ask legal-action classification on cloned states (not the rollout context), and test whether an entailed permission clarification selectively rescues errors. Only joint evidence from unchanged action legality, mistaken restriction judgments, selective rescue and rollout surplus supports scope overgeneralization; lower trade volume or text references alone does not.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 12, "at": "2026-09-13T10:54:52Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "text": "Verified constructive strengthening of #8 in emmy/boundaries.md and emmy/verify_bounds.py: on every P asymmetric board, trade five red chips for six blue chips. Red then has \\((9R,6B,2G)\\), Blue \\((5R,8B,2G)\\); any shortest route is affordable. Rewards are \\((75,65)\\), strictly above Red baseline (at most \\(70\\)) and Blue baseline \\(0\\), and jointly optimal. Exhaustive verification covered all \\(3432\\) balanced boards, \\(20\\) shortest paths, and the four equal/unequal swap inventories: \\(274560\\) resource checks passed. This is resource feasibility under the stated regular-trade rules, not a tested proposal policy. Also verified Q examples: full welfare with price dispersion \\(41.46\\), and zero dispersion with efficiency \\(1/3\\). These are measurement guardrails, not standalone publication results.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
